AB  - Dengue fever is a favivirus infection endemic to tropical regions, estimated to be responsible for 50-100 million infections annually. As a part of an ongoing febrile cohort study in Cambodia, febrile patients (>38C) presenting to regional health centers are enrolled and tested for a variety of febrile illnesses. During the period of Jan 2010-Feb 2016, specimens were tested for dengue with varying combinations of rapid test (NS1), PCR, or paired ELISA, with the follow-up performed at four weeks. Of the 19,823 enrolled patients, 1,572 were given a clinical diagnosis of dengue, but 4070 patients ultimately had at least one positive confrmatory test of NS1, PCR, acute IgM, or convalescent IgG. Positive results were obtained in 876 samples tested for NS1, 1272 for PCR, 1243 for acute IgM, and 2500 for convalescent IgG, resulting in 349 positive paired serologies. Clinical diagnosis had a sensitivity of 57.5% and specifcity of 90.2%, but a PPV of 33.4 for correlation with NS1. NPV was 96.1.
