AB  - Dengue is a flavivirus found largely in areas of the tropics and sub-tropics, placing over half of the world's population at risk. Traditional methods for diagnosis of dengue include detection of serological markers such as IgM and IgG antibodies to dengue. Dengue NS1 is an early marker which can be detected in serum on day 1 after onset of fever and up to day 9. In comparison, an IgM response is not detectable until days 3 to 5. Rapid detection of dengue NS1 antigen is a valuable procedure, as it allows detection of infection prior to seroconversion. Early diagnosis of dengue allows promote implementation of supportive therapy and monitoring which reduces risk of severe complications such as Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (DHF) or Dengue Shock Syndrome (DSS), especially in dengue endemic countries. The Panbio Dengue Early Rapid detects dengue NS1 in sera. The dipstick test was evaluated at two reference laboratories in South-East Asia. The studies used samples from acute fever patients (day
